From 6d7635f856656f2d4c0f95e4512d8a691a2aae41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: medium <medium@debian.debian>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 03:38:54 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] XHTTP client: Start another dial beside a handshake that
 hangs

Every request queued behind a connection waited on its one dial, so a
handshake lost on the way held them all until the 10s bound. A dial now
starts another attempt each time those in flight have gone twice the last
handshake (250ms to 3s, a second before any) without an answer, up to 4,
and takes the first connection made. An error is an answer and is not
retried, and after a dial to a server failed the next one makes a single
attempt until one succeeds, so a path that stays down is not dialed more.

On a stand with 25ms each way and the path blackholed for 3s, sessions
started in the block finished in 3.2s instead of 10.3s, and those started
after it in 0.1s instead of 6.3s. Through a 60s drop the time to recover
moves with the phase of the attempt in flight (45.8s vs 49.1s, 58.0s vs
59.0s, 62.2s vs 60.9s for lifts at 45, 57 and 60s), with 6 to 9 more
connections.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
